 "summary": "Designates uniformed officers of the fire marshal's office of the town of Huntington as peace officers.",
 "title": "Designates uniformed officers of the fire marshal's office of the town of Huntington as peace officers",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A7080",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A7080-2013",
     "printNo": "A7080",
     "session": 2013,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 491\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 3765-A, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to security\n      services in the courts\"\n\nAssembly Bill Number 7080, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT  to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to desig-\n      nating uniformed officers of the fire marshal's office of the town\n      of Huntington as peace officers\"\n\nAssembly Bill Number 9330, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation  to  desig-\n      nating  uniformed court attendants in the town of Ossining, county\n      of Westchester as peace officers\"\n\nAssembly Bill Number 9843, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation  to  desig-\n      nating  uniformed  court  officers  in  the town of New Windsor as\n      peace officers\"\n\nTO THE SENATE:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n\nSenate Bill Number 3894-B, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in  relation  to  peace\n      officer status of special deputy sheriffs appointed by the sheriff\n      of Chautauqua county within the grounds of and properties owned by\n      the Chautauqua Institution\"\n\nSenate Bill Number 7470, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to granting\n      uniformed  members of the bureau of fire prevention of the town of\n      Islip peace officer status\"\n\nSenate Bill Number 7786, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation  to  desig-\n      nating  uniformed court officers of the town of Highlands as peace\n      officers\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  These seven bills would grant peace officer status to fire marshals or\nfire prevention service members in two counties, uniformed  court  offi-\ncers  or  court attendants in four counties, and special deputy sheriffs\nin one county. If designated as peace officers,  these  officials  would\n\nhave many of the same legal powers as police officers. These include the\npowers  to: use force to make arrests, make warrantless arrests, conduct\nwarrantless searches and issue appearance tickets.\n\n  In  2011,  2012, and 2013, I vetoed similar or identical bills, recom-\nmending that the Legislature create a comprehensive process  for  deter-\nmining which categories of officials, on a statewide basis, may need the\npolice  powers granted to peace officers. I again ask the Legislature to\nwork with me to develop such a  comprehensive  approach  to  this  issue\nwithin  the  broader  context of New York State's law enforcement needs,\nrather than addressing the needs of local government units in an ad  hoc\nmanner. For these reasons, I will not approve these bills.\n\n  These bills are disapproved.                (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n                              __________\n",
